# Cloudflare Pages response headers. WO-8.7.
#
# Pages reads this file from the root of what it publishes, and for Planbook that is the
# repository itself: there is no build, no bundler and no output directory (CLAUDE.md). Pages
# consumes the file and never serves it. Syntax is a path pattern on its own line with its
# headers indented under it; a line beginning with # is a comment.
#
# WHY ONLY THESE. Everything else in the app shell is precached by sw.js and answered out of
# Cache Storage, which ignores Cache-Control entirely. What is pinned here is the update path,
# and nothing else needs to be.
#
#   sw.js  — CACHE in sw.js is bumped by hand on every deploy that changes the shell, and
#            `activate` then deletes every cache that is not the new one. None of that starts
#            until the browser fetches a sw.js it has not seen before, so a worker served from
#            an HTTP cache is a teacher opening last week's app with the bump discipline intact
#            and useless. Browsers now mostly bypass their own cache for the worker script; this
#            is the floor under "mostly", and it costs one revalidation.
#
#   the shell document — pinned for the loads that happen before there is a service worker at
#            all: the first visit, and the first one after site data is cleared (which takes the
#            worker with it). BOTH paths are listed because a teacher types the bare origin, so
#            the request that carries the shell arrives at `/`, and a rule written for
#            `/index.html` does not match it.
#
# no-cache is not no-store: the browser still keeps the copy and revalidates it, so a deploy
# that changed nothing costs a 304 rather than a download.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT THE WHOLE STORY, and the part it cannot say is the part that bit first.
# A Cloudflare zone has its own Browser Cache TTL, and on a new zone it defaults to four hours.
# It rewrites Cache-Control on anything the edge caches, which includes .js — so the first
# deploy served /sw.js as `max-age=14400` with this file in place and correct, and the rule
# above simply did not survive the trip. HTML escapes it only because the edge does not cache
# HTML by default (cf-cache-status: DYNAMIC), which is why `/` looked right and hid the problem.
# The zone must be set to **Respect Existing Headers** (Caching -> Configuration) or nothing
# here binds. Measured on the wire, WO-8.7, 2026-08-12. Re-read /sw.js after any zone change:
# a green file in the repo proves nothing about what a teacher's browser was told.
#
# /index.html 308-redirects to / on Pages. The rule below is kept anyway, because it costs
# nothing and the redirect is the host's behaviour rather than ours. sw.js must NOT precache
# that path — see the note on SHELL in sw.js for what happened when it did.

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
